The '''Shendan Ocean''' is the largest body of water on [[Adonia]]. Bounded by the eastern seaboard of [[Fosia]] to the west and the western coast of [[Lurandia]] to the east, it separates the Fosian and Illypnian worlds, and its waters have served as the principal corridor of Fosian maritime expansion since the 15th century.

The deepest known point in any of Adonia's oceans is the [[Mariana Trench]] of the Shendan Ocean, with a depth of 10,911 m. The oldest {{w|oceanic crust}} on the planet is located in the Western Shendan, with an estimated age of about 200 myr. The [[Shendan Plate]] is among the fastest-moving of Adonia's tectonic plates, advancing at a rate of 52–69 mm per year.

==Etymology==
The name derives from the [[Zong language|Zong]] {{nihongo|''Sîn-tan''|神丹}}, meaning "divine cinnabar". The name is attributed to Zong mariners of the eastward expansion era, who observed the sun rising over the open ocean in vivid red hues likened to {{w|cinnabar}}, the mineral of the alchemical elixir tradition. The English exonym "Shendan" is a corruption of the Zong reading.

==Geography==
The Shendan Ocean is bounded to the west by the eastern coast of [[Fosia]], including the [[Jade Peninsula]] of [[Zong]], and to the east by the western coast of [[Lurandia]]. The [[Sanese Archipelago]], including [[Okaiken]] and [[Yanbaru]], lies within its northern waters; Yanbaru in particular occupies a critical maritime chokepoint in the ocean. The northern portion of the ocean connects through the northeastern passages of the [[Jade Sea]], the semi-enclosed commercial artery of eastern Fosia.

==History==
{{main|Great Western Voyage}}
Following the [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1300 – c. 1390), the [[Kingdom of Zong]] was excluded from the western trade routes to [[Illypnia]]. In the 1430s, the Zong court redirected its maritime energies eastward across the Shendan Ocean, beginning the era of Fosian colonization of the New World. The colonies founded on the Lurandian coast — [[Xinhai]] (1456), [[Anping]] (1474), and [[Tamsui]] in [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] (1538) — were the principal products of this expansion, and the convergence of Fosian and Illypnian colonial expansion in Lurandia culminated in the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564.

The ocean later became the setting of the [[Cinc Parets-Macaio galleon trade|Cinc Parets–Macaio galleon trade]], established in 1608, which linked the Shendan Ocean to the western hemisphere and made landfall at the [[Kerama]] islands of [[Yanbaru]] before proceeding to [[Cinc Parets]] in [[Dagit]].

The Shendan Ocean was the primary naval theater of the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), in which a [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]-led expeditionary force crossed the ocean to seize [[Sant Cristòfor]], and of the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), which transferred the city and the [[Balboa Canal]] to Zong control.

